'''Paul S. Eckstein''' {{born|11|September|1963|died|6|June|2023}}, also credited as '''Paul Eckstein''', was an actor who made several appearances on {{s|DS9}} and {{s|VOY}}, always in mask and heavy make-up, including two [[Jem'Hadar]], two [[Hirogen]], and a [[Klingon]]. In addition, he provided voices for the {{video game}} ''[[Star Trek: Armada]]''.

Eckstein made his television debut on {{wt|Law & Order}} starring [[Carolyn McCormick]] in the episode "Consultation", which paired Eckstein with future ''Deep Space Nine'' co-star [[Andrew Robinson]]. The episode also included ''Voyager'' and ''Enterprise'' guest star [[Keith Szarabajka]]. In 1994, in the short film ''Ripple'', Eckstein worked with another ''Deep Space Nine'' co-star, [[Colm Meaney]], as well as [[Megan Gallagher]], [[Ed Lauter]], and [[Robert Picardo]]. Eckstein followed this up with 1995's ''Suspect Device'' with [[Bill Bolender]]. That same year Eckstein played an unnamed paramedic in {{wt|Seven (1995 film)|Seven}} with [[Reg E. Cathey]] and [[Leland Orser]]. 1996 found Eckstein working with [[Lawrence Monoson]] in ''Black Rose of Harlem''. Eckstein then appeared in {{wt|Hoodlum (film)|Hoodlum}} (1997, starring [[Laurence Fishburne]], with [[Ed O'Ross]], [[Mike Starr]], [[Clarence Williams III]] and [[Vanessa Williams]]). Also that year was {{wt|Bella Mafia}}, with [[Gina Philips]] and [[Larissa Laskin]]. 

Outside acting, Eckstein worked as a writer on {{wt|First Wave (TV series)|First Wave}} starring [[Rob LaBelle]], with [[Roger Cross]]. Eckstein also co-wrote. with [[Joe Menosky]], the episode "Zion" for {{wt|The Dead Zone (TV series)|The Dead Zone}}, created by [[Michael Piller]] and [[Shawn Piller]] and starring [[Nicole de Boer]] and [[David Ogden Stiers]], and guest-starring [[Mark Rolston]]. Eckstein co-wrote the episode "Boots on the Ground" for {{wt|Law & Order: Criminal Intent}} in which [[Jeri Ryan]] guested. Eckstein served as story editor on ''Street Time'' for the episode "Born to Kill" guest-starring [[Daniel Dae Kim]]. He was the co-creator of {{wt|Godfather of Harlem}} in which [[Whoopi Goldberg]], [[Paul Sorvino]], [[Ronald Guttman]], [[Geoffrey Blake]] and [[Steve Vinovich]] appeared.

Eckstein died in his sleep in Jamaica, where he was teaching a screenwriting workshop. {{el|hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/paul-eckstein-dead-godfather-of-harlem-1235509821/amp/}}

