'''John Wheeler''' {{born|20|June|1930|died|6|February|2026}} was an actor who played [[Gav]] in the {{s|TOS}} [[TOS Season 2|second season]] episode {{e|Journey to Babel}}. He filmed his scenes on Friday {{d|22|September|1967}} and Monday {{d|25|September|1967}} at [[Desilu Stage 9]].

Prior to acting, during the early 1960s, Wheeler sang as a member of the {{w|The Belafonte Folk Singers}}.

His first TV role following his ''[[Star Trek]]'' appearance was that of a bartender in a 1968 episode of {{wt|Bonanza}} which also featured fellow ''Trek'' guest actor [[George Murdock]]. The following year, Wheeler appeared with fellow ''Original Series'' guest actor [[Victor Tayback]] on an episode of {{wt|TThe Outsider (1968 TV series)|The Outsider}}. He also appeared with [[Frank Corsentino]] and [[Skip Homeier]] on ''Then Came Bronson'' that same year. His other TV credits include ''Green Acres'', ''The Brady Bunch'', {{wt|The Odd Couple (1970 TV series)|The Odd Couple}} (with [[Stanley Adams]], [[Bill Quinn]], and [[Garry Walberg]]), ''[[Mission: Impossible]]'', {{wt|Adam-12}} (1972, with [[Peter Brocco]] and [[Nehemiah Persoff]]), ''Love, American Style'' (including one episode with [[William Windom]]), {{wt|Happy Days}} (with [[Ed Begley, Jr.]]), {{wt|Gunsmoke}}, {{wt|The Rockford Files}}, {{wt|The Golden Girls}}, {{wt|Night Court}} (starring [[John Larroquette]] and also guest-starring [[Gregory Itzin]]), and {{wt|ER (TV series)|ER}}. He also co-starred with {{s|DS9}} regular [[Armin Shimerman]] in an episode of ''Good Grief'', starring [[Wendy Schaal]].

In 1979, Wheeler appeared in the TV special ''The Wild Wild West Revisited'' along with [[René Auberjonois]] and Skip Homeier. In 1983, Wheeler portrayed "Chief Clipper" in the short-lived children's series ''The Kid-a-Littles.'' In 1991, Wheeler played ''[[I Love Lucy]]'' actor {{w|William Frawley}} in {{wt|Lucy & Desi: Before the Laughter}}. Co-starring in this movie were [[Don Keefer]] and [[Alan Oppenheimer]]. Previous TV movie credits include ''With This Ring'' (1978, with [[Jon Lormer]]), {{wt|Kate Bliss and the Ticker Tape Kid}} (1978, with [[Richard Herd]]), and {{wt|Rescue from Gilligan's Island}} (1978, featuring [[Vincent Schiavelli]]).

In film, Wheeler made his debut with an uncredited bit part in [[Elvis Presley]]'s {{wt|Live a Little, Love a Little}} (1968, with [[Paul Sorensen]] and fellow ''Original Series'' guest performer [[Celeste Yarnall]]). This was followed with roles in ''Sweet Charity'' (1969, with [[Ricardo Montalban]], [[Ben Vereen]], and [[Lance LeGault]]), {{wt|Support Your Local Gunfighter}} (1971), {{wt|Mame (film)|Mame}} (1974, starring [[Lucille Ball]], with [[Bruce Davison]] and [[Barbara Bosson]]), {{wt|Bad Georgia Road}} (1977, with [[Gary Lockwood]]), {{wt|Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band (film)|Sgt. Pepper's Lonely Hearts Club Band}} (1978, with [[Keith Carradine]] and [[Carel Struycken]]), {{wt|The Apple Dumpling Gang Rides Again}} (1979, with [[Kenneth Mars]], [[John Arndt]], [[Rex Holman]], [[Ed McCready]], [[Robert Pine]], and [[Nick Ramus]]), ''Banzai Runner'' (1987, starring [[Dean Stockwell]] and [[Biff Yeager]]), and ''Cold Steel'' (1987, with [[Jonathan Banks]] and [[Anne Haney]]). Wheeler can also be seen portraying a lawyer in 1974's ''Big Bad Mama'', starring his ''Original Series'' co-star [[William Shatner]], and as a reporter in {{wt|Apollo 13 (film)|Apollo 13}} (1995, with [[David Andrews]], [[Googy Gress]], [[Max Grodénchik]], [[Clint Howard]], [[Brian Markinson]], [[Andy Milder]], [[Steve Rankin]], and [[Bruce Wright]]).

In 2012, Wheeler presented a special showing of "Journey to Babel" at the Blaisdell Senior Center in Claremont, California. {{el|claremont-courier.com/latest-news/t5059-news-37757/}}

Wheeler died on {{d|6|February|2026}} at his home. {{el|https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/tv/tv-news/john-wheeler-dead-star-trek-mcdonalds-commercial-1236513943}}

